<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad Qavam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a>–<a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Qavam os-Saltaneh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad Qavam, also known as Qavam os-Saltaneh, was an Iranian statesman who served five times as prime minister between 1921 and 1952. In 1946 he persuaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> to withdraw its troops from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> by promising an oil concession, which the Majlis later rejected, and the Soviet-backed governments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a> collapsed. His brief return in 1952 ended in riots that restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-mosaddegh">Mohammad Mosaddegh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-qavam.jpg" alt="Ahmad Qavam" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AhmadQhvamPM1324.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmad Qavam was born on 2 January 1873 into the prominent Mostowfian Ashtiani family. His uncle, Amin al-Dowleh, was a prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and his older brother, Hasan Vossug, served twice as prime minister. Qavam entered the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naser-al-din-shah-qajar">Naser al-Din Shah Qajar</a> early in his career and obtained the title os-Saltaneh during the Constitutional Revolution of 1909. As Dabir-e Hozoor, the private secretary, he wrote the letter signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar">Mozaffar ad-Din Shah</a> accepting the Constitutional Revolution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Qavam was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> in 1918, where he dealt with famine and the Spanish flu pandemic; he was a hardline administrator and banned some of the region's newspapers. In 1921, during the coup d'état in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, he was among the opponents Colonel Pessian was ordered to arrest. When the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zia-ol-din-tabatabaee">Seyyed Zia'eddin Tabatabaee</a> fell and others refused the premiership in an unstable situation, Qavam, just released from Ishratabad prison, was offered it and accepted. He later crushed the revolt of Colonel Pessian with the aid of Reza Pahlavi. His terms also included the invitation to Arthur Millspaugh to assist with the government's finances, the riots of 1942 over economic hardship and the Tripartite Treaty of 1942 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><p>On 26 January 1946 the Majlis voted him prime minister by 52 to 51, hoping that, as the largest property owner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, he could best resolve the Soviet-inspired rebellion in the occupied province. He sent the Iranian delegation at the UN to negotiate directly with the Soviet delegation and flew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to talk with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>. The Soviets did not withdraw by 2 March 1946, the date the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hartford-1638">Tripartite Treaty</a> set, and Mohammed Mossadegh rebuked this. Qavam then arranged a deal granting the Soviets an oil concession in the north, conditional on approval by the Majlis after the elections, and Soviet troops began to withdraw.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The new Majlis voted against the concession at once. The episode earned Qavam the congenial title The Old Fox, and it brought strong opposition from the newspaper Atesh and its editor Mehdi Mir Ashrafi, and from Mard-i Imruz and its editor Mohammad Masud, who argued publicly that he should be killed. Qavam died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> in 1955 at the age of 82. He was survived by his second wife and his only son, Hossein. Twice, according to the sources, he played a significant part in preventing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from annexing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s northern provinces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmad Qavam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Prime Minister <b>Ahmad Qavam</b> promised the Soviets an oil concession, Soviet troops withdrew in May 1946.</blockquote><a href="/state/azerbaijan-peoples-government">Azerbaijan People's Government</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.69,51.39&amp;year=1926" aria-label="Tehran on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5848 874 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ahmad Qavam"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6298 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6298 1144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.69,51.39&amp;year=1926">What was here in 1926? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
